WEBVTT

00:00:05.080 --> 00:00:11.680
Some animals are notoriously difficult to see.

00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:12.280
Can you see it?

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:18.600
Many only come out at night...

00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:19.720
No, we can't come in here now.

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:23.880
Others hide in holes or up trees...

00:00:22.920 --> 00:00:24.160
It's a bit deeper there, Gordon.

00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:29.320
And some just like to keep their heads down...

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:43.120
But this doesn't deter the devoted and plucky folk who make up Britain's secret wild army.

00:00:45.640 --> 00:00:57.080
Four animals, four amateur naturalists, four inspiring stories.

00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:14.240
Gordon, what have we got singing over there at the moment?

00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:19.280
It takes more than a spot of rain to put these three intrepid gentlemen off their regular wildlife mission.

00:01:18.640 --> 00:01:20.760
We were waiting for that.

00:01:25.320 --> 00:01:37.080
In the depths of a Devonshire wood are 50 nest boxes, all carefully mapped and meticulously numbered, and all of them need inspecting.

00:01:36.320 --> 00:01:40.840
This is more like jungle exploration!

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:54.000
Leading this dedicated band is retired biology teacher Tom Maddock.

00:01:53.240 --> 00:01:57.240
This is going on this stone, then I can open the box.

00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:00.680
I always think this is like Christmas, cos you never quite know what you're gonna find.

00:02:00.640 --> 00:02:04.680
And, in fact, what we've got is a brood of blue tits.

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:08.000
That was box number one and 49 to go!

00:02:10.680 --> 00:02:14.480
Something's been in there, a slug...?

00:02:14.680 --> 00:02:19.840
I can see, um, shreds of plant material, so I'm gonna take this one down.

00:02:32.040 --> 00:02:40.000
Wonderful, isn't it?

00:02:41.880 --> 00:02:45.880
Look at that.

00:02:46.160 --> 00:02:52.200
What we have here is a torpid dormouse.

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:52.960
There we are.

00:02:54.600 --> 00:02:58.160
This is the reason for our quest.

00:03:01.520 --> 00:03:04.560
It's a delightful find, but it's not just for personal pleasure.

00:03:03.760 --> 00:03:10.840
Tom and his friends are part of the National Dormouse Monitoring scheme, volunteers who are qualified to handle and study this rare and endearing mammal.

00:03:10.640 --> 00:03:15.080
No other rodent spends half its life sleeping.

00:03:14.720 --> 00:03:24.520
These will sleep for six months of the year, and they will normally start to wake up about midday, they're late risers.

00:03:24.840 --> 00:03:28.720
He doesn't want to show me his special parts.

00:03:27.880 --> 00:03:29.320
Hee-hee!

00:03:28.440 --> 00:03:31.400
He's very coy, this one.

00:03:33.240 --> 00:03:36.360
I would say...that we have...

00:03:35.440 --> 00:03:37.280
We have a male here.

00:03:38.720 --> 00:03:40.960
You can see a tiny penis.

00:03:42.640 --> 00:03:45.240
So what I'm gonna do next is weigh the animal...

00:03:48.040 --> 00:03:50.400
This one's 19.8.

00:03:51.760 --> 00:03:52.800
A good weight.

00:03:56.320 --> 00:04:03.600
Tom's love of wildlife is part of a great British tradition.

00:04:02.720 --> 00:04:13.120
For centuries, like-minded amateur naturalists have watched and recorded everything that moves and grows, making Britain the best-studied place on Earth.

00:04:12.920 --> 00:04:18.480
Like many others, Tom was first inspired when he was just a boy, and it's all down to this man, HG Hurrell.

00:04:25.840 --> 00:04:29.440
Hurrell's family is a family of naturalists.

00:04:28.600 --> 00:04:33.960
His younger son, Kenneth, his daughter Elaine, who runs a section of the Bristol Naturalists Society.

00:04:33.360 --> 00:04:38.040
Their mutual love of wildlife forms a deep family bond of understanding.

00:04:39.560 --> 00:04:41.240
Hurrell was himself an amateur naturalist.

00:04:40.440 --> 00:04:44.320
His knowledge and interest was infectious.

00:04:43.480 --> 00:04:50.080
HG came to school once and showed films at school.

00:04:49.240 --> 00:04:52.680
I was trapped by that, really.

00:04:51.840 --> 00:04:54.840
And he was to me something of an idol.

00:04:54.280 --> 00:04:58.800
And I thought, "This is what natural history's all about."

00:04:58.320 --> 00:05:07.520
HG died in 1981, but his daughter Elaine, now in her 70s, is still a good friend, and mentor, to Tom.

00:05:06.680 --> 00:05:09.440
Hello, Tom.

00:05:09.200 --> 00:05:10.600
Come inside.

00:05:12.040 --> 00:05:14.440
Right, we've managed to complete the whole check.

00:05:13.520 --> 00:05:20.960
There's our first dormouse, in number nine, nicely torpid, so that made for ease of handling.

00:05:22.320 --> 00:05:32.640
It means a lot to Elaine that Tom is carrying on the dormouse work she and her father started back in the 1950s.

00:05:31.880 --> 00:05:33.080
We've got three in here.

00:05:32.240 --> 00:05:38.600
I suppose my father was very interested in recording anyway in all kinds of observations.

00:05:37.840 --> 00:05:43.760
His diary was kept for something like 60 years.

00:05:42.920 --> 00:05:57.960
You know, it's extremely interesting now to read them, of course, because he said, "I saw a beautiful mouse tonight, absolutely perfect, "lovely tail, and I had a really good view of it."

00:05:57.280 --> 00:06:05.920
And that's what we were doing, was watching for dormice behaving naturally in the wild.

00:06:06.440 --> 00:06:10.560
Through their dedicated dormice watching, the amateur naturalists made a significant discovery.

00:06:10.240 --> 00:06:21.360
"Late October, I happened to walk under a large hazel bush "about 100 yards from our house, "and found that other creatures besides squirrels had shown interest in the nuts.

00:06:20.640 --> 00:06:27.920
"I picked up over 30 shells from which the kernels had been extracted through a hole at one side.

00:06:27.080 --> 00:06:31.440
"This could not possibly be the work of squirrels.

00:06:30.760 --> 00:06:32.840
"It appeared even at a glance to be the work of mice."

00:06:34.440 --> 00:06:37.080
They were very good carpenters, dormice, I always remember that.

00:06:36.280 --> 00:06:45.320
And they have a tiny chiselling effect on the edge of the hole.

00:06:47.280 --> 00:06:55.200
The discovery of these distinctive calling cards mean that you no longer have to see dormice to know they're there.

00:06:56.400 --> 00:07:01.880
For the first time, it was possible to produce a dormouse map of Britain.

00:07:01.160 --> 00:07:05.960
The survey was, when you look back on it, was great fun.

00:07:05.120 --> 00:07:07.400
It was hard work as well, but...

00:07:06.720 --> 00:07:12.720
Jumping in the bramble bushes to look for nuts!

00:07:14.960 --> 00:07:16.600
Ah, you can see its front feet quite well...

00:07:17.720 --> 00:07:20.920
Ah, it's on the move, I'm gonna have a job to sex this one, I think.

00:07:22.160 --> 00:07:25.680
The total weight now is 55.3...

00:07:24.960 --> 00:07:29.840
So that's a gram or so more than that female.

00:07:29.880 --> 00:07:37.760
All this weighing, sexing and measuring goes to the People's Trust For Endangered Animals.

00:07:37.720 --> 00:07:42.280
Sadly, dormice are on their list.

00:07:42.280 --> 00:08:08.320
It's an amazing creature that has been in this country for thousands of years, and up till, well, perhaps maybe...only about 50 years ago, we started to get to grips with how this animal behaved, and what it was doing, and because we now have more data about dormice than maybe any other small mammal in this country, any effects of global warming might first appear in a creature like this, which has a completely different strategy from all the others.

00:08:08.600 --> 00:08:20.160
It's vital that amateurs like these keep an eye on animals like dormice, and it's lucky their sleeping habits allow us to see them easily.

00:08:19.280 --> 00:08:29.400
But hidden in a wooded valley in Gloucestershire, other mammals have chosen a less accessible, but much grander place for a bedroom.

00:08:31.000 --> 00:08:37.440
Woodchester estate was bought by William Leigh in 1845.

00:08:37.000 --> 00:08:43.679
He commissioned architects to build a forbidding gothic mansion, but the house was never finished.

00:08:42.960 --> 00:08:46.160
It was mysteriously abandoned 23 years later.

00:08:50.040 --> 00:09:01.920
Visitors have reported sightings of a headless horseman, and even a floating coffin!

00:09:01.160 --> 00:09:08.240
No wonder it has the reputation of being one of the most haunted houses in Britain!

00:09:08.840 --> 00:09:17.200
And up in the ancient rafters are some residents who certainly add a frisson to the place - but to see them, you have to wait until after dark.

00:09:23.960 --> 00:09:32.720
The horseshoe bats are famous for their noses.

00:09:33.800 --> 00:09:40.680
It just blasts its echo-location sounds through its nostrils into what is like a parabolic reflector, it's like a torch beam.

00:09:40.120 --> 00:09:48.200
Then they get these beautifully focused beams of ultrasound, which make them incredibly good at distinguishing fine detail.

00:09:47.440 --> 00:09:57.160
They can discriminate between different types of insects while there are flying, in ways that are very difficult to understand.

00:09:58.120 --> 00:10:08.440
Despite being the largest and longest-living bat in Britain, its numbers have plummeted by 90% over the last 100 years, making it one of our rarest species.

00:10:09.000 --> 00:10:13.000
But this is the first file I had to do my project.

00:10:12.440 --> 00:10:18.960
We did basic descriptions of the habitat...

00:10:18.120 --> 00:10:21.240
Roger Ransome was the ultimate schoolboy naturalist.

00:10:20.440 --> 00:10:26.040
I started working on bats in 1956 as part of a school project.

00:10:25.720 --> 00:10:33.800
I felt that this was a really good area to get into because there was very little known about the natural history of bats.

00:10:33.000 --> 00:10:35.000
It was known that bats live a long time.

00:10:34.080 --> 00:10:39.640
They actually can live up to 40 years.

00:10:42.080 --> 00:10:48.360
We had heard that there were lots of bats coming out of Woodchester mansion.

00:10:47.600 --> 00:10:53.720
And we made an attempt to get in while we were at school.

00:10:52.880 --> 00:11:09.520
The man who had the lease on the mansion wasn't too keen on sixth-formers wandering around, and it wasn't until 1959 that I persuaded him that, really, I was an upright and honest person...

00:11:09.000 --> 00:11:14.680
Roger's schoolboy persistence has continued for 51 years.

00:11:16.640 --> 00:11:25.240
His study of the bats in Woodchester has been going on longer than any study of any mammal in the whole world!

00:11:26.040 --> 00:11:29.720
When I started, I was doing all the basic biometric measurements that I still do today.

00:11:29.000 --> 00:11:37.480
We're now through something like 10 generations, it's just keeping it going consistently for that period of time which makes it become valuable.

00:11:36.560 --> 00:11:46.960
The computer says 107 bats went out.

00:11:46.440 --> 00:11:50.920
Nowadays, the computer is the saving grace.

00:11:50.160 --> 00:11:54.080
I could not process data without it.

00:11:53.320 --> 00:11:59.000
That's where Michael, my grandson, comes in, cos he's my IT adviser.

00:11:58.280 --> 00:11:59.560
Are yours coming out, Gemma?

00:12:01.880 --> 00:12:06.000
We've only got three out so far.

00:12:06.520 --> 00:12:11.520
Every week, Roger and his army of volunteers use a mass of technology to monitor the bats.

00:12:13.160 --> 00:12:20.360
Ted and Gemma are...have swung the cameras around, so that they are counting the bats flying out to feed.

00:12:19.960 --> 00:12:26.880
If the bats go out when it's very, very bright, we know they're diet-stressed.

00:12:25.920 --> 00:12:32.560
If they go out when it's very dark, we know they've got plenty to eat, and they're fine.

00:12:33.880 --> 00:12:35.240
This one...

00:12:34.400 --> 00:12:42.720
Well, this is to look for behaviour patterns between animals that we know are related to each other.

00:12:42.480 --> 00:12:46.320
And the associations seem to be mainly between the females.

00:12:45.640 --> 00:13:01.360
This is a female-dominated society, er, and what we have is a series of matrilines, lines of bats that go back to a single female.

00:13:00.720 --> 00:13:07.960
And all together, we've got something like 15-17 matrilines in the colony, so it's a large number.

00:13:07.040 --> 00:13:10.120
The more I find out, the more there is to find out.

00:13:09.960 --> 00:13:12.440
The complexity of this animal is just utterly amazing.

00:13:12.600 --> 00:13:19.920
It's been around 40 million years, and, er, we're pretty recent arrivals on the scene.

00:13:20.120 --> 00:13:28.480
They have an incredibly complex social organisation, vocal communications, which we're only just beginning to understand.

00:13:27.640 --> 00:13:33.600
Right, so, I'm going through this hole...

00:13:34.320 --> 00:13:38.720
Once the bats have left the roost, Roger can get in.

00:13:37.760 --> 00:13:42.520
Well, how else can you collect your weekly bat droppings?

00:13:44.280 --> 00:13:47.520
This yellow sheet is beneath the attic space that we were looking at.

00:13:48.720 --> 00:14:05.960
I'm gonna take them back to my house, dry them all out, and then we'll treat them in solutions, and we're left with the skeletons of the insects, which we can recognise under the microscope.

00:14:05.080 --> 00:14:06.360
And all jolly good fun.

00:14:05.480 --> 00:14:11.440
We do it week in, week out, and we see how the diet changes.

00:14:10.680 --> 00:14:13.760
That's quite a lot, really, for one week.

00:14:12.880 --> 00:14:18.040
They're feeding well at the moment.

00:14:20.480 --> 00:14:26.400
Roger has contributed to 34 scientific papers.

00:14:25.640 --> 00:14:34.040
And in 2002, he co-wrote the European Action Plan, which gives better protection to greater horseshoe bats.

00:14:33.200 --> 00:14:34.480
They do say scientists have their most productive work by the time they are 30 or 40.

00:14:33.520 --> 00:14:34.720
I think that depends on the type of work you're doing.

00:14:38.720 --> 00:14:44.760
To make generalisations about what's going on, you do need long periods of time.

00:14:43.960 --> 00:14:58.000
When you've got a bat that lives 30 years, then really you should do 60 years to be sure that the first 30 years is the same as the second 30!

00:14:57.200 --> 00:15:00.520
But will Roger ever feel like he's done enough?

00:14:59.680 --> 00:15:10.760
First of all, I thought when you got to 40, you were past doing these sorts of things, then I thought, "Well, perhaps when you get to 50, you're past it."

00:15:09.960 --> 00:15:15.800
And at the moment my wife thinks that it's high time that I hung up my boots.

00:15:15.000 --> 00:15:21.680
To be fair to her, it will have to come one day.

00:15:22.680 --> 00:15:26.320
Determination is the watchword for mammal lovers.

00:15:25.720 --> 00:15:29.080
Their dedication spans decades.

00:15:29.160 --> 00:15:33.840
These long-term studies often provide the information others need when planning our modern world.

00:15:32.880 --> 00:15:39.440
Thank goodness amateur naturalists have the stamina and commitment to keep going.

00:15:39.440 --> 00:15:48.320
I want to be someone who knows as much as possible about European brown hares.

00:15:47.680 --> 00:15:52.840
And I'd much rather be here even though it's raining than sitting in an office.

00:15:52.040 --> 00:15:56.880
I'm not just standing here getting wet.

00:15:56.120 --> 00:16:01.160
I'm pursuing something I really want to do.

00:16:01.560 --> 00:16:12.200
This pretty suburban garden in Hertfordshire just 15 miles from London is surprisingly close to where Gill Turner likes to spend most of her time.

00:16:17.800 --> 00:16:22.640
It's 5.30 in the morning and Gill is already on her way.

00:16:23.000 --> 00:16:30.920
She is one of only a handful of amateur naturalists in the UK studying the secretive brown hare.

00:16:30.520 --> 00:16:40.000
I prefer to come out in the early morning because the light's better then and there's more chance of seeing them still grazing from the night before.

00:16:39.400 --> 00:16:43.800
I've been aware of the hares in my area for many years.

00:16:43.040 --> 00:16:54.320
I started to wonder what they were up to basically and try and understand why their numbers were dwindling.

00:16:55.160 --> 00:17:01.240
It was the Romans who introduced hares to this country about 2,000 years ago.

00:17:01.280 --> 00:17:06.359
They were once widespread throughout Britain but have now disappeared from many places.

00:17:07.200 --> 00:17:23.599
When you see something running at...that looks like a very large rabbit running at 45mph across in front of you, you can't help but think Britain's fastest mammal, you know, that's incredible.

00:17:23.880 --> 00:17:33.760
For 15 years, Gill has immersed herself in the life of hares, honing her field skills, so she can get as close as possible.

00:17:32.920 --> 00:17:42.080
We're just coming up to the meadow where I know the hares are so if possible could we be quiet and creep over there without making too much noise?

00:17:41.880 --> 00:17:43.000
(Thank you. )

00:17:57.480 --> 00:17:58.520
Keep this way.

00:18:02.880 --> 00:18:05.000
Can you see down there?

00:18:04.280 --> 00:18:09.120
There's two hares down there... together.

00:18:09.880 --> 00:18:11.400
Did you see that at all?

00:18:11.560 --> 00:18:12.960
Oh, dear.

00:18:15.880 --> 00:18:21.800
Sometimes it takes the eye of a true amateur to spot what's hiding in the field.

00:18:25.800 --> 00:18:27.680
Can you see them?

00:18:28.640 --> 00:18:32.520
I can see two hares.

00:18:32.840 --> 00:18:35.840
They're both grazing.

00:18:36.080 --> 00:18:40.160
I think they're siblings, part of a group of three.

00:18:41.920 --> 00:18:46.200
I suppose there's a possibility that... ..as they get older they'll attract... ..bucks.

00:18:51.360 --> 00:18:58.400
Probably in this length of grass, the first the first thing you'll see are the ears.

00:19:05.440 --> 00:19:13.080
When I'm photographing them, and if they come really close to me, I have a job to keep my camera steady because it's so exciting.

00:19:13.800 --> 00:19:23.800
Each time I think, "Oh, I've obviously got a brilliant photograph this time."

00:19:22.960 --> 00:19:31.800
Gill's photos help her to understand behaviour and identify who's who.

00:19:32.120 --> 00:19:39.600
If it's a buck and a doe, it's possible that they'll completely ignore me because they're fully occupied in what they're doing.

00:19:38.840 --> 00:19:47.480
And with youngsters, they're so oblivious, especially if they haven't had any contact with humans anyway but they're easy to photograph.

00:19:50.680 --> 00:19:55.920
I think they're animals that not many people know much about.

00:19:55.240 --> 00:20:01.160
And there's so much more to a hare than just looking like a big rabbit, you know.

00:20:00.280 --> 00:20:04.960
Their life is complex, it really is.

00:20:07.920 --> 00:20:17.400
These beautiful and entrancing animals have declined dramatically over the last 100 years, so more information is needed to help them survive the modern world.

00:20:16.560 --> 00:20:21.280
Gill's observations go to her local records office and it's vital studies like these continue through generations.

00:20:26.560 --> 00:20:33.040
My mentor was Tony Holly.

00:20:33.360 --> 00:20:40.040
I wrote to him and he called me almost straight away.

00:20:40.200 --> 00:20:53.600
And I was overwhelmed, actually, it was wonderful that somebody as important as him should take an interest in somebody who hadn't got any scientific background.

00:20:52.760 --> 00:20:54.320
It was great.

00:20:53.520 --> 00:20:56.680
He really encouraged me.

00:20:55.880 --> 00:21:05.960
When he died, I felt very much alone in my work because I'd had somebody to report to.

00:21:09.000 --> 00:21:20.320
Sometimes when I'm on my own and I'm wet and I'm cold... ..and I haven't seen a hare, I think to myself, "Why am I doing this?

00:21:19.920 --> 00:21:21.600
Does anybody really care?"

00:21:20.760 --> 00:21:35.680
And I've got friends who think I'm mad, but I like to think that I've got enough field experience and I've spent enough time watching the hares without interfering with them, that I have gained some knowledge and possibly seen things that other people don't see.

00:21:36.920 --> 00:21:42.880
Hares, like most mammals, find themselves at the rough end of human development.

00:21:42.040 --> 00:21:48.120
Intensive agriculture, housing, industrialisation and roads all take their toll.

00:21:48.600 --> 00:21:53.400
One mammal in particular was pushed right to the brink of extinction.

00:21:54.720 --> 00:21:57.720
Rare, elusive... nocturnal...

00:22:04.640 --> 00:22:14.720
We're going to go to the otter loo, or what I call the otter loo, which is a pile of stones in the stream, which I check every day.

00:22:14.520 --> 00:22:19.440
They leave a nice juicy heap of pooh, if I'm lucky, a nice spraint.

00:22:19.240 --> 00:22:31.520
That means that I can tell the frequency with which this stream is used by otters which gives some indication of the level of population.

00:22:34.200 --> 00:22:36.480
It's very difficult to see an otter.

00:22:35.720 --> 00:22:38.440
I've never seen one here.

00:22:37.600 --> 00:22:44.000
If I wanted to see an otter at the otter loo, I'd have to sit on the bridge for half the night.

00:22:43.960 --> 00:22:47.640
So this is the otter loo.

00:22:46.800 --> 00:22:50.200
This is the side stream of the River Tone.

00:22:50.680 --> 00:22:52.480
Oh, my goodness!

00:22:51.680 --> 00:22:58.680
They must have known you were coming because that set of footmarks there, that's otter padding going upstream.

00:22:58.400 --> 00:23:05.960
I washed that sand yesterday and there wasn't any otter padding at bedtime last night, so I'm very pleased.

00:23:05.800 --> 00:23:07.040
On my little stream.

00:23:15.160 --> 00:23:25.160
At the moment I'm quite excited because I think I've got a bitch and cub and so I'm checking to see whether this is one woozle or two weasels, or whatever Christopher Robin said.

00:23:24.320 --> 00:23:35.640
But I think an otter would go one, two and be off and the fact that we've got more, I'm hoping that that is the cub and that's the mother there.

00:23:35.880 --> 00:23:37.240
And this spraint...

00:23:36.560 --> 00:23:45.760
That's a very typical otter spraint, a sort of black shapeless, crinkly object... ..which it gives me great pleasure to see.

00:23:46.840 --> 00:23:54.960
Otters signal to other otters by leaving their pooh on a rock as a smelly warning that this bit of river is already occupied.

00:23:54.080 --> 00:23:57.760
Trespassers are not welcome.

00:24:01.040 --> 00:24:05.640
Why otters and not any other species?

00:24:04.800 --> 00:24:09.720
Because all other species are inferior.

00:24:13.040 --> 00:24:18.760
But it takes an expert to be an otter.

00:24:19.200 --> 00:24:25.920
They really are in control of themselves, in control of their environment, they're just a very exceptional grade of animal.

00:24:31.240 --> 00:24:35.960
James's admiration came out of a long but unusual relationship with otters.

00:24:38.040 --> 00:24:39.960
We were totally involved in otter hunting.

00:24:39.080 --> 00:24:45.040
Father was master of the otter hounds and so was I eventually.

00:24:44.240 --> 00:25:02.480
In those days, there was no form of conservation other than the hunts, really, who paid money to the farmers where otters were found who gave Christmas presents to gamekeepers to not trap the otters to destruction, so the hunters were the conservationists of the day.

00:25:02.040 --> 00:25:22.240
When the decline came and pesticides started to harm the otters, it was the hunts that drew attention to this and the hunts that provided most of the data which enabled people to get to grips with it.

00:25:22.160 --> 00:25:39.120
They're cleaner to the eye, they don't smell as much and if we want to have some wildlife we need a new generation of people to come along.

00:25:41.440 --> 00:25:52.760
James's passion for otters led him to run the Somerset Otter Group, an 80-strong band of amateur naturalists who routinely check a particular stretch of river.

00:25:53.320 --> 00:25:55.360
By putting all the data together, he gets a unique insight into the local otters.

00:25:55.840 --> 00:26:00.440
We gradually build up a picture of evidence, a bit like lots of little creatures build up the Great Barrier Reef.

00:25:59.680 --> 00:26:05.040
Little bits, little bits, little bits all add together.

00:26:05.240 --> 00:26:13.080
It may not seem as if you're making a lot of difference but I have had one or two things which I think were of importance.

00:26:12.240 --> 00:26:35.280
We picked up a dead otter and sent it away for analysis and it turned out to hold a world record of dieldrin at a time about six, seven years after dieldrin had been banned and it was traced back with the help of some experts to a government cloth factory where they were using dieldrin.

00:26:34.440 --> 00:26:41.160
Farmers help.

00:26:40.280 --> 00:26:44.320
Birdwatchers are a very great source of information.

00:26:43.720 --> 00:26:48.200
We put it all together and gradually we're getting most of the jigsaw.

00:26:49.640 --> 00:26:53.720
In some ways, it's a thankless passion, not least because James never sees an otter on his land.

00:26:54.080 --> 00:26:56.960
Does it take dedication?

00:26:56.200 --> 00:27:02.000
I suppose it does take dedication to study a species that you never see.

00:27:03.080 --> 00:27:09.160
This nomadic creature comes mysteriously through my garden at night.

00:27:08.400 --> 00:27:09.760
It doesn't give twopence about us.

00:27:08.920 --> 00:27:14.280
It's just going through the river as it has done for hundreds of years.

00:27:13.320 --> 00:27:14.800
Our presence is nothing to the otter, but the otter's presence is everything to me.

00:27:18.960 --> 00:27:24.560
Otters, like all our animals, need people who care deeply about them.

00:27:24.000 --> 00:27:31.360
Our British wildlife needs a healthy environment and it doesn't take much to upset the balance.

00:27:31.880 --> 00:27:34.080
Otters live right on the cusp of what it is possible for them to live.

00:27:33.200 --> 00:27:36.720
If the otters go, it means the streams are in a mess.

00:27:35.920 --> 00:27:37.400
It means the fish are in a mess.

00:27:36.560 --> 00:27:40.960
It means the dragonflies are in a mess.

00:27:40.520 --> 00:27:46.960
It means that kingfisher that just came past us won't come past.

00:27:46.200 --> 00:27:47.360
If the otters can't hack it, nobody can hack it.

00:27:46.400 --> 00:27:50.280
We've got to have clean rivers.

00:27:50.360 --> 00:27:56.120
The hallmark of the amateur British mammal lover is dedication - and thank goodness.

00:27:56.880 --> 00:28:10.640
Without them, many of our mammals might quietly disappear without most of us noticing.

00:28:09.840 --> 00:28:20.120
They're about two millimetres across.

00:28:22.840 --> 00:28:25.960
There's not enough...

00:28:25.200 --> 00:28:28.200
The less than glamorous but fascinating...

00:28:27.320 --> 00:28:28.480
Eugh!

00:28:30.080 --> 00:28:33.240
And the minibeasts that keep our rivers alive.

00:28:32.280 --> 00:28:39.560
What's he doing in there?!
